329
00:51:28.130 --> 00:51:42.750
Mark Blackman: So the outputs of the network are across multiple streams. And really the major piece of this feature is bringing all of those into one what we call block stream.

330
00:51:43.170 --> 00:51:55.290
Mark Blackman: and furthermore, each block in this stream represents one or more rounds of consensus, and when I say, represents

331
00:51:55.410 --> 00:52:02.899
Mark Blackman: it, not only has all the transactions, all the events, all the safe States, but it also has a proof

332
00:52:03.130 --> 00:52:18.169
Mark Blackman: within that block that is self-contained so that you could verify that block independently without having to gather signature files as you do today or other files.

333
00:52:18.280 --> 00:52:22.979
Mark Blackman: So it's it's very powerful that these are self verifying.

334
00:52:24.347 --> 00:52:31.100
Mark Blackman: So what does what does this all bring us? a. a 1 unified stream? As I mentioned

335
00:52:31.290 --> 00:52:41.729
Mark Blackman: B. The State change data. This enables, for the 1st time in Hedera's history, a non-consensus node

336
00:52:41.870 --> 00:53:04.010
Mark Blackman: service or node type to be able to maintain consensus alongside the network. And this this capability brings us a lot of new functionality and primitives, such as state proofs, which is foundational for a lot of other things. We want to do here on Hedera.

337
00:53:04.870 --> 00:53:09.320
Mark Blackman: As I mentioned, it is verifiable.

338
00:53:09.450 --> 00:53:19.269
Mark Blackman: Basically, there is an aggregated signature included in this block that you could verify and know just from this one proof

339
00:53:19.570 --> 00:53:24.030
Mark Blackman: that a majority of the node has signed off on this block.

340
00:53:24.984 --> 00:53:33.849
